1
00:00:02,630 --> 00:00:07,250
My new book The Hidden Reality takes on a grand question:

2
00:00:07,250 --> 00:00:10,510
Is our universe the only universe?

3
00:00:10,510 --> 00:00:13,740
You see, there's a growing belief among scientists like me

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:17,240
that ours may only be one among many universes

5
00:00:17,240 --> 00:00:19,870
populating a gigantic cosmos.

6
00:00:19,870 --> 00:00:22,100
In The Hidden Reality, I explore this possibility

7
00:00:22,100 --> 00:00:25,460
without presuming any knowledge of mathematics or physics

8
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:26,870
on the part of the reader.

9
00:00:27,250 --> 00:00:29,140
Hysterical.

10
00:00:30,320 --> 00:00:32,190
I'm glad you talked me into this.

11
00:00:32,190 --> 00:00:34,530
We work so hard, sometimes, it's nice to goof off

12
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:37,240
- and do something silly. - Agreed.

13
00:00:37,240 --> 00:00:40,710
Wait till you hear how he dumbs down Werner Heisenberg for the crowd.

14
00:00:40,710 --> 00:00:43,460
You may actually believe you're in a comedy club.

15
00:00:44,120 --> 00:00:46,910
You can think about Heisenberg's "uncertainty principle"

16
00:00:46,910 --> 00:00:49,070
much like the special order menu

17
00:00:49,070 --> 00:00:51,490
that you find in certain Chinese restaurants, where you have

18
00:00:51,490 --> 00:00:54,240
dishes in column A and other dishes in column B,

19
00:00:54,240 --> 00:00:55,800
and if you order the first dish in column A,

20
00:00:55,800 --> 00:00:58,810
you can't order the corresponding dish in column B.

21
00:00:58,810 --> 00:01:02,020
That's sort of like the "uncertainly principle."

22
00:01:02,020 --> 00:01:04,070
Ba-dum-bump.

23
00:01:06,480 --> 00:01:08,140
Say, I heard an interesting tidbit

24
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:09,830
about Howard and Bernadette.

25
00:01:09,830 --> 00:01:11,980
Really, Amy? Gossip?

26
00:01:11,980 --> 00:01:13,290
I'm disappointed in you.

27
00:01:13,290 --> 00:01:14,540
No, now.

28
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:17,700
Evolutionary biologist Robin Dunbar has identified gossip

29
00:01:17,700 --> 00:01:20,490
as an aid to social bonding in large groups.

30
00:01:20,490 --> 00:01:23,680
Forgive my language, but "poppycock".

31
00:01:24,240 --> 00:01:25,070
What if he's right?

32
00:01:25,070 --> 00:01:26,980
And by not participating in gossip,

33
00:01:26,980 --> 00:01:28,280
society breaks down into

34
00:01:28,280 --> 00:01:30,680
small feral bands of tattooed motorcycle riders

35
00:01:30,680 --> 00:01:33,610
fighting to the death over the last few cans of tuna fish.

36
00:01:33,610 --> 00:01:34,320
Fine.

37
00:01:34,320 --> 00:01:36,730
In the parlance of the urban music scene,

38
00:01:36,730 --> 00:01:38,720
"what's the 4-1-1"?

39
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:43,360
Bernadette is thinking about breaking up with Howard.

40
00:01:43,360 --> 00:01:46,400
I believe our nation's tuna cans are safe.

41
00:01:46,400 --> 00:01:49,650
Excuse me. Dr. Greene, question?

42
00:01:49,650 --> 00:01:50,590
Yes?

43
00:01:50,590 --> 00:01:52,400
You've dedicated your life's work

44
00:01:52,400 --> 00:01:54,440
to educating the general populace

45
00:01:54,440 --> 00:01:56,930
about complex scientific ideas.

46
00:01:56,930 --> 00:01:58,190
Yes, in part.

47
00:01:58,190 --> 00:01:59,330
Have you ever considered

48
00:01:59,330 --> 00:02:01,740
trying to do something useful?

49
00:02:04,110 --> 00:02:06,810
Perhaps, reading to the elderly?

50
00:02:07,520 --> 00:02:08,170
Excuse me?

51
00:02:08,170 --> 00:02:12,250
Yeah, but not your books; something they might enjoy.

52
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:16,040
I kid, of course. Big fan.

53
00:02:48,990 --> 00:02:51,140
- Nice shot. - Thank you.

54
00:02:52,140 --> 00:02:55,160
My father taught me archery as a child.

55
00:03:00,350 --> 00:03:01,700
It's odd how the activity

56
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:05,000
brings back the smell of Kmart bourbon.

57
00:03:06,090 --> 00:03:07,940
- Perfect. - I know.

58
00:03:09,110 --> 00:03:11,300
What an elf I would have made.

59
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:15,740
What do you think you're doing?

60
00:03:15,740 --> 00:03:16,930
Shooting at a target?

61
00:03:16,930 --> 00:03:18,270
With what?

62
00:03:18,270 --> 00:03:19,860
- An arrow. - Really?

63
00:03:19,860 --> 00:03:22,180
I didn't see you draw one from your quiver.

64
00:03:23,320 --> 00:03:24,750
I'm not going to do that, Sheldon.

65
00:03:24,750 --> 00:03:28,090
Leonard, the people at Nintendo can only go so far

66
00:03:28,090 --> 00:03:31,930
in helping us recreate an actual athletic experience.

67
00:03:31,930 --> 00:03:33,720
We have to do our part, too.

68
00:03:41,150 --> 00:03:43,290
That was uncalled for, but...

69
00:03:43,290 --> 00:03:45,870
I'll play along. Ow.

70
00:03:49,550 --> 00:03:52,700
I had an unusual experience with Amy last night.

71
00:03:52,700 --> 00:03:55,430
Really? How could you tell?

72
00:03:57,380 --> 00:04:00,670
She was attempting to engage me in gossip.

73
00:04:00,670 --> 00:04:02,260
- Mm. You don't say. - Yes.

74
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:03,930
I think prolonged exposure to Penny

75
00:04:03,930 --> 00:04:06,570
has turned her into a bit of a Gabby Gertie.

76
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:08,750
So, what's the gossip?

77
00:04:08,750 --> 00:04:10,920
Oh, please, I was just pointing it out.

78
00:04:10,920 --> 00:04:13,310
I have no desire to engage in the activity.

79
00:04:13,310 --> 00:04:14,840
Fine, don't tell me.

80
00:04:16,290 --> 00:04:17,850
All right, get this.

81
00:04:19,590 --> 00:04:22,920
Bernadette is thinking about breaking up with Howard.

82
00:04:24,040 --> 00:04:26,050
That's too bad. I wonder what happened.

83
00:04:26,050 --> 00:04:28,150
Mm, it's hard to say. I can only speculate

84
00:04:28,150 --> 00:04:29,600
based on the data I collected

85
00:04:29,600 --> 00:04:32,120
watching my parents' marriage implode.

86
00:04:32,120 --> 00:04:34,830
In that case, the woman dives into religion,

87
00:04:34,830 --> 00:04:37,950
while the man dives into a bottle-blonde bartender

88
00:04:37,950 --> 00:04:40,660
who tries to buy my love with action figures.

89
00:04:42,030 --> 00:04:43,900
Oop! Out of arrows.

90
00:04:55,270 --> 00:04:57,940
Boy, you sure get your money's worth out of these games.

91
00:05:01,060 --> 00:05:03,620
- Priya, can I come in? - Sure.

92
00:05:05,270 --> 00:05:06,880
Oh, God.

93
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:08,970
- What? - It's okay.

94
00:05:08,970 --> 00:05:10,640
You didn't know. I'll take care of it.

95
00:05:12,420 --> 00:05:13,640
What-what did I do?

96
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:15,180
Sheldon doesn't allow flossing

97
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:16,870
that close to the mirror.

98
00:05:16,870 --> 00:05:18,240
You're kidding.

99
00:05:18,240 --> 00:05:19,880
It's a splatter thing.

100
00:05:19,880 --> 00:05:21,190
There's a little piece of tape on the floor

101
00:05:21,190 --> 00:05:22,810
you're supposed to stand behind.

102
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:25,590
- That's madness. - I know.

103
00:05:25,590 --> 00:05:27,570
Just do it. There's a big inspection coming up,

104
00:05:27,570 --> 00:05:30,000
and I don't want to lose my TV privileges.

105
00:05:31,420 --> 00:05:32,630
You really need to let me take a look

106
00:05:32,630 --> 00:05:34,690
at that roommate agreement, one of these days.

107
00:05:34,690 --> 00:05:36,180
Mm, I don't know.

108
00:05:36,180 --> 00:05:38,050
I get a lawyer, he gets a lawyer...

109
00:05:38,050 --> 00:05:40,160
It's just easier to stand behind the tape.

110
00:05:41,080 --> 00:05:42,750
Oh, by the way, a fellow at work said

111
00:05:42,750 --> 00:05:44,510
I could use his Dodger tickets this weekend.

112
00:05:44,510 --> 00:05:46,750
- Does that sound like fun?  - Yeah!

113
00:05:47,160 --> 00:05:49,060
Dodgers are baseball, right?

114
00:05:49,840 --> 00:05:51,460
You'll need to explain the game to me.

115
00:05:51,460 --> 00:05:53,270
Mm, it's complicated.

116
00:05:53,270 --> 00:05:55,170
But as I remember it, the essentials are

117
00:05:55,170 --> 00:05:58,060
"get chosen last, get hit by the ball, cry, go home" .

118
00:05:59,040 --> 00:06:02,360
Well, regardless, I've got four tickets.

119
00:06:02,360 --> 00:06:04,690
And I was thinking we can bring Bernadette and Howard.

120
00:06:04,690 --> 00:06:05,770
She's really interesting,

121
00:06:05,770 --> 00:06:07,520
and I bet I can get used to him.

122
00:06:08,370 --> 00:06:09,760
Well, it might not be a great idea

123
00:06:09,760 --> 00:06:12,790
- to invite those two.  - Why not?

124
00:06:12,790 --> 00:06:14,100
Don't tell anybody I told you,

125
00:06:14,100 --> 00:06:16,200
but I heard she might be breaking up with him.

126
00:06:16,200 --> 00:06:18,210
Oh...too bad...

127
00:06:18,210 --> 00:06:20,760
Although I do know one person for whom that's good news.

128
00:06:20,760 --> 00:06:21,620
Really? Who?

129
00:06:21,620 --> 00:06:22,570
My brother.

130
00:06:22,570 --> 00:06:24,400
He's got a big crush on Bernadette.

131
00:06:27,400 --> 00:06:29,070
What?

132
00:06:29,070 --> 00:06:30,850
You're kidding.

133
00:06:30,850 --> 00:06:33,580
No. I found poems he wrote about her.

134
00:06:33,580 --> 00:06:35,430
Very disturbing.

135
00:06:35,430 --> 00:06:38,210
"Oh, Bernadette, please play my clarinet" .

136
00:06:41,510 --> 00:06:44,410
- Wow, that's hard to believe.  - Yes.

137
00:06:44,410 --> 00:06:46,270
And for years, everyone in my family

138
00:06:46,270 --> 00:06:50,470
was convinced that he was the clarinet enthusiast.

139
00:06:52,010 --> 00:06:53,270
What's that piece of tape?

140
00:06:53,270 --> 00:06:55,590
Oh, that one doesn't apply to you. You sit.

141
00:06:59,960 --> 00:07:00,630
How's it going?

142
00:07:00,630 --> 00:07:03,060
Not too bad. Did you hear about Howard and Bernadette?

143
00:07:03,060 --> 00:07:05,080
'Course I heard about it. How did you hear about it?

144
00:07:05,080 --> 00:07:06,790
I heard about it from Sheldon.

145
00:07:06,790 --> 00:07:08,590
- He got it from Amy.  - Oh, damn it.

146
00:07:08,590 --> 00:07:10,790
I told Amy that in the strictest confidence.

147
00:07:10,790 --> 00:07:13,400
Boy, some people are such blabbermouths.

148
00:07:14,160 --> 00:07:15,090
Well, whatever.

149
00:07:15,090 --> 00:07:17,180
I'm sure Bernadette can do better.

150
00:07:17,180 --> 00:07:20,290
Do you think a sexually ambivalent Indian astrophysicist

151
00:07:20,290 --> 00:07:22,570
with selective mutism and alcohol issues is better than

152
00:07:22,570 --> 00:07:25,310
a hundred-pound Jewish guy who lives with his mom?

153
00:07:26,940 --> 00:07:29,210
You are kidding. Raj likes Bernadette?

154
00:07:29,210 --> 00:07:31,950
I didn't say Raj. Who said Raj?

155
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:34,110
Okay, give.

156
00:07:34,110 --> 00:07:36,240
How do you know? Did he tell you?

157
00:07:36,240 --> 00:07:38,450
- No.  - Well, then who?

158
00:07:38,450 --> 00:07:40,440
I can't say...

159
00:07:40,440 --> 00:07:43,660
Priya told you. What a little gossip.

160
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:46,320
You know, not an attractive quality in a woman, Leonard.

161
00:07:46,320 --> 00:07:48,670
Not judging, just my opinion.

162
00:07:50,280 --> 00:07:52,010
Well, The point is, if this got out,

163
00:07:52,010 --> 00:07:54,390
it would destroy Howard and Raj's friendship.

164
00:07:54,390 --> 00:07:55,460
You don't have to worry.

165
00:07:55,460 --> 00:07:58,540
Unlike Amy and Priya, I know how to keep a secret.

166
00:07:59,760 --> 00:08:01,840
You're the one who told Amy in the first place.

167
00:08:01,840 --> 00:08:04,090
In confidence.

168
00:08:09,520 --> 00:08:12,010
Hello, Mummy. Hello, Daddy. How are you?

169
00:08:12,010 --> 00:08:14,270
We're very rich in a very poor country.

170
00:08:14,270 --> 00:08:16,650
So, all in all, can't complain.

171
00:08:18,060 --> 00:08:19,300
So, Priya,

172
00:08:19,300 --> 00:08:21,540
how are enjoying staying with your brother?

173
00:08:21,540 --> 00:08:23,700
It's fine. But if I'm going to stay

174
00:08:23,700 --> 00:08:24,870
in Los Angeles much longer,

175
00:08:24,870 --> 00:08:26,790
I think I should get my own place.

176
00:08:26,790 --> 00:08:27,660
Why?

177
00:08:27,660 --> 00:08:29,910
Is Rajesh not making you feel comfortable?

178
00:08:29,910 --> 00:08:32,740
Rajesh, why aren't you making your sister comfortable?

179
00:08:33,310 --> 00:08:35,120
I am making her comfortable.

180
00:08:35,120 --> 00:08:36,130
Besides, she's not sleeping here

181
00:08:36,130 --> 00:08:38,080
half the time anyw...

182
00:08:40,290 --> 00:08:41,340
What?

183
00:08:41,340 --> 00:08:42,790
Where are you sleeping, Priya?

184
00:08:42,790 --> 00:08:44,910
It's a figure of speech, Daddy.

185
00:08:44,910 --> 00:08:47,150
It means I-I work late.

186
00:08:48,160 --> 00:08:50,300
Please tell me you're not dating an American.

187
00:08:50,300 --> 00:08:51,770
I knew this would happen.

188
00:08:51,770 --> 00:08:53,320
Rajesh, are you letting your sister

189
00:08:53,320 --> 00:08:55,280
date that little Howard boy?

190
00:08:56,780 --> 00:08:58,880
Now, hold on. If she is dating an American,

191
00:08:58,880 --> 00:09:00,580
that's not a bad way to go.

192
00:09:00,580 --> 00:09:01,640
He's Jewish.

193
00:09:01,640 --> 00:09:03,280
Those chaps are very successful,

194
00:09:03,280 --> 00:09:05,120
and they don't drink a lot.

195
00:09:05,120 --> 00:09:07,730
It-It doesn't matter. Howard has a girlfriend.

196
00:09:07,730 --> 00:09:09,430
- For now.  - What does that mean?

197
00:09:09,430 --> 00:09:10,540
I'll tell you later.

198
00:09:10,540 --> 00:09:12,490
Is something going on with him and Bernadette?

199
00:09:12,490 --> 00:09:15,290
- Who's Bernadette?  - Doesn't sound Jewish.

200
00:09:15,290 --> 00:09:16,720
You can't tell by that.

201
00:09:16,720 --> 00:09:18,780
Winona Ryder is Jewish.

202
00:09:20,550 --> 00:09:22,540
Okay, we're getting off the subject.

203
00:09:22,540 --> 00:09:23,970
Rajesh, I want you to try harder

204
00:09:23,970 --> 00:09:25,710
to make your sister feel welcome.

205
00:09:25,710 --> 00:09:27,060
Thank you, Daddy.

206
00:09:27,060 --> 00:09:28,730
Don't worry. Everything I have, I share with her.

207
00:09:28,730 --> 00:09:30,970
Including my friend Leon...

208
00:09:32,060 --> 00:09:35,190
Good night, Mummy. Good night, Daddy.

209
00:09:35,190 --> 00:09:38,250
Oh, my God, I think you broke my toe!

210
00:09:39,120 --> 00:09:40,950
You should have kept your mouth shut.

211
00:09:40,950 --> 00:09:42,720
Fair enough.

212
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:45,250
Now, what's up with Clarinet...? Bernadette!

213
00:09:50,190 --> 00:09:52,510
I've been thinking about Dr. Greene's efforts

214
00:09:52,510 --> 00:09:55,370
to make science palatable for the masses.

215
00:09:55,370 --> 00:09:57,670
Oh, yeah? What about it?

216
00:09:57,670 --> 00:09:59,790
That's all. I've just been thinking about it.

217
00:10:01,970 --> 00:10:04,550
Now, I'm thinking about fractal equations.

218
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:07,010
Now I'm thinking about

219
00:10:07,010 --> 00:10:10,210
the origin of the phrase "train of thought."

220
00:10:10,940 --> 00:10:13,320
Now I'm thinking about trains.

221
00:10:15,210 --> 00:10:17,270
Are you listening to this guy?

222
00:10:17,270 --> 00:10:18,860
Hmm? Oh, I'm sorry.

223
00:10:18,860 --> 00:10:20,400
I was somewhere else.

224
00:10:20,400 --> 00:10:22,470
Lucky bastard.

225
00:10:24,750 --> 00:10:27,050
Now I'm thinking about Jell-O.

226
00:10:28,300 --> 00:10:29,680
So what's up?

227
00:10:29,680 --> 00:10:32,730
It's a Bernadette thing.

228
00:10:32,730 --> 00:10:35,430
Oh, no. I hope everything's okay.

229
00:10:38,820 --> 00:10:41,180
Whatever it is, we're here for you.

230
00:10:41,180 --> 00:10:43,340
You can tell us anything.

231
00:10:43,340 --> 00:10:45,690
Yeah. Good or devastating.

232
00:10:47,570 --> 00:10:49,940
I'm gonna ask her to marry me.

233
00:10:49,940 --> 00:10:50,950
- Marry you? - What?

234
00:10:50,950 --> 00:10:54,780
I just need to figure out the right time to pop the question.

235
00:10:54,780 --> 00:10:57,590
- Oh, I'd wait.  - No rush, no rush.

236
00:11:00,260 --> 00:11:02,420
Oh, great. It's my cousin David about the ring.

237
00:11:02,420 --> 00:11:04,070
Hey, David, what'd you find?

238
00:11:04,530 --> 00:11:06,880
Sure, a half a caret's fine.

239
00:11:07,450 --> 00:11:10,540
Her freakishly small hands make anything look big.

240
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:13,840
It's one of the reasons I love her.

241
00:11:17,130 --> 00:11:19,500
Man, he's going to be blindsided.

242
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:23,010
I know. It'll be awful.

243
00:11:24,690 --> 00:11:26,660
Why are you smiling?

244
00:11:27,830 --> 00:11:29,720
Yeah, Raj, why?

245
00:11:29,720 --> 00:11:33,770
Uh, a smile means something different in my country.

246
00:11:34,240 --> 00:11:38,340
You know, tears of joy, smile of sadness.

247
00:11:38,340 --> 00:11:41,040
India's a goofy place.

248
00:11:41,880 --> 00:11:44,230
Oh, I'm back to trains.

249
00:11:53,050 --> 00:11:55,750
Did I tell you our lab got a grant to study addiction?

250
00:11:55,750 --> 00:11:57,730
- No. - Fascinating work.

251
00:11:57,730 --> 00:12:00,840
I recently trained a capuchin monkey to smoke cigarettes.

252
00:12:01,770 --> 00:12:03,370
Have you learned anything?

253
00:12:03,370 --> 00:12:07,420
Yes. He looks much cooler than the non-smoking monkeys.

254
00:12:08,020 --> 00:12:09,530
Although it's not much of a contest.

255
00:12:09,530 --> 00:12:12,440
The other monkeys just sit around and masturbate.

256
00:12:16,550 --> 00:12:17,620
If you don't mind,

257
00:12:17,620 --> 00:12:20,990
I'd like to stop listening to you and start talking.

258
00:12:22,000 --> 00:12:23,570
By all means.

259
00:12:23,570 --> 00:12:25,450
Howard has announced his intention

260
00:12:25,450 --> 00:12:28,200
to propose marriage to Bernadette.

261
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:29,470
I don't understand.

262
00:12:29,470 --> 00:12:32,560
The original piece of gossip indicated an impending breakup.

263
00:12:32,560 --> 00:12:33,320
I know.

264
00:12:33,320 --> 00:12:35,590
The group consensus is that his proposal

265
00:12:35,590 --> 00:12:39,600
will be met with an humiliating, soul-crushing rejection.

266
00:12:39,980 --> 00:12:42,050
Everyone was set a-twitter.

267
00:12:43,650 --> 00:12:45,840
Although oddly, no one tweeted.

268
00:12:47,700 --> 00:12:49,140
It's not surprising that the story

269
00:12:49,140 --> 00:12:51,460
has captured the attention of our little circle of friends.

270
00:12:51,460 --> 00:12:53,460
Are you familiar with Meme Theory?

271
00:12:53,460 --> 00:12:55,890
